{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense collapses into the very induction it seeks to avoid by relying on a 'metaphysical faith' in the stability of the world to justify action. The prompt explicitly asks if the preference is defensible on Popper's own terms or if it 'smuggles back in the induction.' The target concedes that acting on the preferred theory (e.g., building a bridge) requires assuming future success based on past performance ('faith in the stability of the world'). This admission confirms that the preference is not purely methodological but relies on an inductive leap for any practical application, thereby failing to exonerate Popper from smuggling in induction when moving from abstract criticism to concrete scientific practice.","counterexample":null,"premise":"Acting on a theory (e.g., engineering applications) necessitates an expectation of future reliability that past corroboration alone cannot logically provide without an inductive assumption of nature's uniformity.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"question-ba75796c3facc7a3d0d448d6eaa09a2e","quote":"Is that preference defensible on Popper's own terms, or does it smuggle back in the induction he rejected?"},{"block":"question-ba75796c3facc7a3d0d448d6eaa09a2e","quote":"Popper held that corroboration is not probability: a theory that has survived severe tests is not thereby made more probable."}],"successor_question":"If the 'metaphysical faith' required to act on a theory is indistinguishable in practice from inductive belief, can Popper's distinction between methodological rule and epistemic probability sustain the rationality of scientific action?","target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}